    {
      "idx": 0,
      "label": "1",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "RLM means recursive language model. It can\ndelegate parts to helper models and use\ncode.\n\nHere we isolate a simpler building block:\none model choosing actions and using\ntools. The game checks actual items, not\npersuasive text.\n\nWe test training examples and the division\nof work between model and code."
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "We fine-tune Qwen3-4B using small LoRA\nadapters. A training row pairs the task,\ninventory and history with the next\naction.\n\nBoth teachers are programs; both show\nlookups. One uses known recipes to choose\nits route; the other discovers the route\nthrough lookups.\n\nSame 32 tasks and 366 steps, but different\nhistories and text lengths."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Each repeat means training both methods\nagain with a different random seed. Each\nmodel makes 32 test attempts.\n\nThe chart shows 5 versus 15 and 3 versus\n10. Task-group uncertainty intervals favor\ndiscovery in both repeats.\n\nChanged recipes: 4 versus 6 and 1 versus\n8, out of 16. The smaller difference\nremains uncertain."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Code uses a recipe already in the public\nhistory. It preserves the chosen item and\namount; it does not pick subgoals or\nreveal hidden recipes.\n\nFour comparisons: 6 to 12, 8 to 10, 9 to\n14, 9 to 13. Paired wins: 6/2/5/4; no\nlosses.\n\nShared goals and generator, not 64\nindependent tasks. The smallest gain\nremains uncertain."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "We have two promising effects, not a\ngeneral solution to planning. Teaching\npackages differ in several ways; the\nmechanism is not isolated.\n\nThe queued test compares both teachers\nwith and without code assistance on\nadditional goals and recipe settings.\n\nA second domain and later helper-call\ntests would strengthen the research."
    }
  ]
}
